WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.921 --> 00:00:06.390
HARVEY TUCH:
Afternoon, everybody.

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:08.490
And welcome to a
technical deep dive

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:11.950
into service mesh networking.

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:14.850
The session today is
going to be in four parts.

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:17.370
Act one, the service
mesh networking,

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:19.890
in which I will provide an
overview of this service mesh

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:22.500
and describe the role in
which the Envoy proxy plays

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.510
in the service mesh data plane.

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:26.940
In part two, my
colleague Arun well

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:29.730
talk about Google's managed
control plane for the service

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:32.009
mesh Traffic Director.

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:34.500
This was mentioned in
the keynotes yesterday

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.970
and at a fantastic
session by [INAUDIBLE],,

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:39.922
which I recommend watching
on YouTube if you haven't.

00:00:39.922 --> 00:00:44.860
And finally, Larry Peterson, CTO
of Open Networking Foundation,

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:46.940
will describe some
novel use cases

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:50.570
for the service
mesh and L2 and L3,

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:52.700
where in telecommunications
industry,

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:55.820
the service mesh concepts
have been applied.

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:59.520
Finally, we'll wrap
up with some Q&amp;A.

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:01.660
By way of introduction,
I'm Harvey Tuch.

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:02.460
I'm a Googler.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:05.830
I work on Envoy platform
issues at Google.

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:07.347
I'm also an open
source contributor

00:01:07.347 --> 00:01:08.430
in Envoy and a maintainer.

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:11.280
And I'm super passionate
about Envoy and its role

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.330
in the service mesh.

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:16.990
Why do we care about the
service mesh and Envoy?

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:20.650
Part of this is driven by this
move towards microservices.

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:22.690
This is pretty uncontroversial.

00:01:22.690 --> 00:01:24.880
There's industry-wide
shift that's

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:28.570
undergoing right now from
managing your applications

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:30.670
like pets, to cattle,
or in the case

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:33.760
of this slide, an adorable
basket full of puppies.

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:37.150
And, you know, this
has brought huge wins

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:41.620
for development velocity,
orchestration, making

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:43.420
our systems more dynamic.

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:47.650
But, on the other hand, there
is a perceived and actual cost

00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:51.430
in terms of complexity
at the networking level.

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:53.260
Enter the service mesh.

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:57.260
In the service mesh, we extract
away all this network plumbing

00:01:57.260 --> 00:01:59.560
and the nuts and bolts.

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:02.140
And instead, we start
reasoning at the granularity

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:04.870
of applications
and microservices.

00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:08.949
We think in terms of application
security, service discovery,

00:02:08.949 --> 00:02:11.920
and observability.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:14.330
How does this work?

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:17.650
Well, one common topology
for the service mesh

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:19.930
is the sidecar proxy.

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:21.550
We inject into a
container running

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:25.250
alongside the applications an
additional process of binary,

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:27.760
which is essentially
gateway to the service

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:30.200
mesh for that application.

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:34.360
It contains all this the
sort of network function

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:36.700
goodies that are required to
participate in the service

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:37.420
mesh--

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:39.940
from service discovery,
to authentication,

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:45.040
authorization, rate
limiting, tracing, and so on.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:47.808
All these network
functions are factored out

00:02:47.808 --> 00:02:49.600
of the individual
applications so that they

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:52.480
don't need to be
reimplemented multiple times

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:56.350
in each different
language, or framework,

00:02:56.350 --> 00:03:00.060
or microservice that
you might be writing.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:01.633
There are alternative
topologies.

00:03:01.633 --> 00:03:03.300
We won't talk too
much about them today.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:05.820
But just to give
you an idea, you

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:08.430
can also imagine
running a library which

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:09.760
contains this functionality.

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:12.180
And this is typified
by gRPC-LB, which

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:15.210
is a fantastic example
of client side,

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:17.310
look at side load
balancing, where

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:19.800
instead of hopping through
an additional process,

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:23.790
you instead remain in
process and you use your IPC

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:27.450
libraries, essentially, your
gateway to the service mesh.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:31.650
This has advantages in terms
of orchestration and reduced

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:33.900
performance overhead.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:36.440
Another example,
alternative and service mesh

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:42.050
that is sometimes seen, is the
middle proxy configuration,

00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:45.920
in which legacy applications
in brownfields and deployments

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:50.187
are bridged to the service
mesh via an external proxy.

00:03:50.187 --> 00:03:51.770
They may not be
running in containers.

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:54.530
You can't re-link these
against some library

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:58.140
to provide the service
mesh capabilities.

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:04.570
Envoy is the leading open
source service mesh proxy.

00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:08.800
It runs as a sidecar proxy,
as we saw a few slides ago.

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:11.760
And we're going to just dive
into some of the details

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:14.140
of what Envoy is about.

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:16.980
So a good starting place is
what is Envoy, concretely?

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:19.170
And if we look at
its architecture

00:04:19.170 --> 00:04:21.660
its design philosophy,
its a code-based built

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:25.880
for performance predictability
and low overhead.

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:28.220
It's written in c++.

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:30.150
It's a midsized project.

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:32.150
It's designed-- it's
written in such a low level

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:34.610
language because we care
about things like tail

00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:38.690
latency and minimizing overhead
on the fast path in the data

00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:39.530
plane.

00:04:39.530 --> 00:04:42.110
We care about reducing memory
consumption-- we're running

00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:44.650
in a Docker environment.

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:46.380
We also care about
security, reliability.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:48.600
Being a low level
language, it's also vital

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:50.430
that you then apply
best practices when it

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:52.140
comes to software engineering.

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:55.060
We have incredibly
high test coverage.

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:57.690
I developed a community which
has borrowed its best practices

00:04:57.690 --> 00:05:00.150
from companies like Google,
Lyft, in the industry,

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:05.190
to produce reliable, and
stable, and secure software.

00:05:05.190 --> 00:05:06.760
Envoy is not just a proxy.

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:08.260
And, in a few slides
down, I'm going

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:10.900
to talk about Envoy
as a platform.

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:12.850
So keep that in mind.

00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:15.520
It does have a bunch of sort
of goodies built into it,

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:17.220
including advanced
load balancing,

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:21.360
and health-checking algorithms,
support for modern protocols

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:22.200
first--

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:24.210
so it doesn't have
too much legacy cruft

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:26.310
from ancient network protocols--

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:29.610
and integrates well with
other folks and players

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:32.370
in the service mesh
infrastructure space--

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:36.490
systems such as Prometheus,
or Datadog, or Zipkit.

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:39.410
So we've covered,
like, what Envoy is.

00:05:39.410 --> 00:05:41.800
And the question is
like who is Envoy.

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:43.300
What is this community?

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:44.800
Who's behind it?

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:47.290
Who uses Envoy in production?

00:05:47.290 --> 00:05:50.970
This slide sort of captures some
of the contributions to Envoy

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:55.400
at [INAUDIBLE] by
number of lines of code.

00:05:55.400 --> 00:05:57.537
Envoy's providence is Lyft.

00:05:57.537 --> 00:05:59.870
It was created by the project
founder Matt Klein at Lyft

00:05:59.870 --> 00:06:02.870
to solve the service mesh
networking needs of Lyft.

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:06.383
it was open source roughly
two and a half years ago.

00:06:06.383 --> 00:06:08.800
Since then, there's been a
significant uptake in interest,

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:09.883
in particular from Google.

00:06:09.883 --> 00:06:12.550
And this long tail
of developers--

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:14.443
Google's the green
bar you can see here

00:06:14.443 --> 00:06:15.610
that's second from the left.

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:17.590
Lyft is the pink.

00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:20.380
And there's a huge number
of companies which make

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:23.030
our community so awesome today.

00:06:23.030 --> 00:06:26.740
They contribute all kinds of
integrations and improvements

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:28.570
to the core Envoy proxy.

00:06:28.570 --> 00:06:32.060
These are traditional
enterprise companies.

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:34.540
They're Sas providers.

00:06:34.540 --> 00:06:37.280
And they're startups.

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:40.310
If we look here at just the
lines of code over time,

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:41.930
clearly Envoy's
become much larger.

00:06:41.930 --> 00:06:43.790
But it's also become
much more diverse

00:06:43.790 --> 00:06:45.860
in terms of its contributors.

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:49.100
We start on the left,
back in the end of 2016

00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:51.240
with just Lyft in
that sea of pink.

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:54.290
In the lot 2017 in the middle,
you can see Google on the left

00:06:54.290 --> 00:06:57.080
were the main
contributors on the right.

00:06:57.080 --> 00:06:59.600
The community is now
pretty well balanced

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:03.320
between this long tail of the
rest of the community, Google,

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:05.970
and Lyft.

00:07:05.970 --> 00:07:08.790
Envoy is used much more widely
in production and justice

00:07:08.790 --> 00:07:10.680
development in the
community though.

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:12.660
You can see here some
familiar brand names

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:14.250
you'll find on the
Envoy landing page

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:16.560
for folks who are running
on volume production

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:19.140
and are proud to say so.

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:21.720
There is also a huge
number of other folks

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:24.870
who are using Envoy in
these kinds of roles,

00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:27.705
for example Sas or cloud
infrastructure providers,

00:07:27.705 --> 00:07:33.300
and cloud service providers
who might be using it either

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:34.020
directly.

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:37.140
Or who might be using it because
is embedded in other products.

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:39.330
Envoy is, for example,
ships with Istio.

00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:43.230
Anyone using Istio is,
by virtue, using Envoy.

00:07:43.230 --> 00:07:47.100
Why is Envoy being so successful
and being used so widely?

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:50.200
Well, part of this is
because Envoy is a platform.

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:51.060
It's versatile.

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:54.090
It can meet the needs
of various use cases

00:07:54.090 --> 00:07:57.190
and be reconfigured
as needed to be.

00:07:57.190 --> 00:07:58.940
This platform-like
nature, arises

00:07:58.940 --> 00:08:00.560
from two aspects of Envoy.

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:03.680
First it's control
plane, which is

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:06.830
built around a set of
open gRPC-based protocols

00:08:06.830 --> 00:08:10.400
called xDS, allowing
Envoy to integrate first

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:13.730
with well-known control
planes like Istio and Traffic

00:08:13.730 --> 00:08:18.860
Director, but also with
custom in-house network

00:08:18.860 --> 00:08:22.910
infrastructure that
might need Envoy

00:08:22.910 --> 00:08:28.240
to fit within its existing
configuration pipeline.

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:30.760
Envoy's data plane
is also extensible.

00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:33.220
That is, all the traffic
flowing through Envoy

00:08:33.220 --> 00:08:36.400
can be interposed on by
extensions that you can write

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:38.390
and that the community
has written to add

00:08:38.390 --> 00:08:40.720
in all kinds of functionality.

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:43.960
This slide lists
about roughly a dozen

00:08:43.960 --> 00:08:48.190
or so-plus extensions
that exist today in Envoy.

00:08:48.190 --> 00:08:51.730
These range from the meat and
potatoes of its TCP and HTTP

00:08:51.730 --> 00:08:53.060
filters.

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:55.780
These filters
provide the ability

00:08:55.780 --> 00:08:58.970
to do things like transcoding
between different protocols,

00:08:58.970 --> 00:09:03.940
let's say gRPC and JSON rest,
or to apply additional control

00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:06.330
over the stream of traffic,
for example rate limiting

00:09:06.330 --> 00:09:08.050
or fault injection.

00:09:08.050 --> 00:09:09.700
There's also sort
of advance extension

00:09:09.700 --> 00:09:13.240
features for things like
replacing transport security.

00:09:13.240 --> 00:09:16.380
We actually have our current
PR for introducing an extension

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:19.810
point for hardware
security modules.

00:09:19.810 --> 00:09:22.930
Envoy's extensions go
beyond just a data plane.

00:09:22.930 --> 00:09:25.445
It's data extensions
can we exist

00:09:25.445 --> 00:09:26.570
for things like monitoring.

00:09:26.570 --> 00:09:28.910
So you can integrate with
pretty much any monitoring

00:09:28.910 --> 00:09:30.520
system that exists.

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:32.570
Envoy also ships with
batteries included.

00:09:32.570 --> 00:09:36.110
So it already has integrations
with many of the systems

00:09:36.110 --> 00:09:40.370
that you might care about,
for example Prometheus.

00:09:40.370 --> 00:09:44.333
Today, extensions are written
in c++ primarily and you

00:09:44.333 --> 00:09:46.250
statically link them
against the Envoy binary.

00:09:46.250 --> 00:09:48.920
But there's actually a
very promising trend,

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:52.160
which we're seeing right now, in
which the extensions are being

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:55.400
offered in dynamic languages
like Lua injected in runtime

00:09:55.400 --> 00:09:57.860
to Envoy via the control plane.

00:09:57.860 --> 00:10:01.190
This makes unvoiced
programmable data plane.

00:10:01.190 --> 00:10:03.730
And, in the future,
we anticipate

00:10:03.730 --> 00:10:05.930
that WebAssembly
support will arrive.

00:10:05.930 --> 00:10:08.240
And we'll be able
to basically write

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:10.580
extensions in any
language that you want,

00:10:10.580 --> 00:10:15.360
and deliver it's dynamically
to a running Envoy process.

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:18.030
Its control plane is
built around this alphabet

00:10:18.030 --> 00:10:20.220
soup of protocols,
which we call xDS.

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:22.320
These sub protocols
allow things like service

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:25.830
discovery, load balancing
assignments, load reporting,

00:10:25.830 --> 00:10:29.808
delivery of route configuration,
health checks, and so on.

00:10:29.808 --> 00:10:31.350
They're canonically
defined and built

00:10:31.350 --> 00:10:34.910
around gRPC streaming
and protocol buffers.

00:10:34.910 --> 00:10:37.020
And we have REST,
YAML and JSON variants

00:10:37.020 --> 00:10:39.205
for folks who prefer
those technologies.

00:10:39.205 --> 00:10:40.830
They have a range of
consistency models

00:10:40.830 --> 00:10:43.860
depending upon the use case
and how your control plane

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:45.817
and management serve
as a configured.

00:10:45.817 --> 00:10:47.900
We view these protocols,
though, as not just being

00:10:47.900 --> 00:10:50.660
Envoy's control plane, but
being a sort of a lingua franca

00:10:50.660 --> 00:10:52.850
for network proxies in general.

00:10:52.850 --> 00:10:55.220
That is, that they are
the universal data plane

00:10:55.220 --> 00:10:56.700
API for L4 and L7.

00:10:59.570 --> 00:11:03.170
Once you have sort
of an ecosystem, sort

00:11:03.170 --> 00:11:05.280
of management service
and control plane which

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:07.250
are capable of
running Envoy's, you

00:11:07.250 --> 00:11:10.580
can run pretty much anything
that implements these APIs.

00:11:10.580 --> 00:11:12.980
This might include,
for example, gRPC-lb,

00:11:12.980 --> 00:11:16.460
which is adopting the xDS APIs
for slient side load balancing

00:11:16.460 --> 00:11:19.257
for those clients [INAUDIBLE]
be service meshes.

00:11:19.257 --> 00:11:21.590
It could include in the future
things like hardware load

00:11:21.590 --> 00:11:23.970
balances or other
software proxies,

00:11:23.970 --> 00:11:25.965
which choose to adopt
the Envoy's API.

00:11:25.965 --> 00:11:27.590
And there is an active
effort right now

00:11:27.590 --> 00:11:32.750
in the community on making
these APIs structured

00:11:32.750 --> 00:11:35.930
and versioned appropriately
to enable splitting off

00:11:35.930 --> 00:11:37.430
concerns which are
specific to Envoy

00:11:37.430 --> 00:11:40.160
from their universal nature.

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:41.750
Another way to
conceptualize this

00:11:41.750 --> 00:11:47.710
is that this is really the
analogy of open flow of P4

00:11:47.710 --> 00:11:50.300
for L4 and L7.

00:11:50.300 --> 00:11:50.990
So this is it.

00:11:50.990 --> 00:11:53.450
With open flow, you
can reconfigure,

00:11:53.450 --> 00:11:59.180
let's say, IP routing via an
open flow controller with SDN.

00:11:59.180 --> 00:12:05.542
You can do the same with Envoy
and its data plane via xDS.

00:12:05.542 --> 00:12:07.000
I'd like to just
finish off briefly

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:10.540
by touching on the relationship
between Envoy and Istio.

00:12:10.540 --> 00:12:15.190
This is one of the most
famous associations

00:12:15.190 --> 00:12:16.540
that exists for Envoy.

00:12:16.540 --> 00:12:19.910
And it functions pretty much
as a standard sidecar proxy.

00:12:19.910 --> 00:12:23.530
This is also an interesting case
study in Envoy's extensibility.

00:12:23.530 --> 00:12:27.460
First, it's managed by the
Istio control plane called

00:12:27.460 --> 00:12:30.670
pilots over any xDS protocols.

00:12:30.670 --> 00:12:35.560
And it also features a number
of specific Istio extensions

00:12:35.560 --> 00:12:37.720
that are linked against
the Envoy binary

00:12:37.720 --> 00:12:40.540
to allow it to add runtime
interposed on network traffic

00:12:40.540 --> 00:12:44.290
and funnel them to via hard
PCS to the mixer service, which

00:12:44.290 --> 00:12:46.180
is part of the Istio runtime.

00:12:46.180 --> 00:12:48.610
And there, they can enforce
additional policy checks,

00:12:48.610 --> 00:12:51.190
and logging, and
telemetry, and so on.

00:12:51.190 --> 00:12:54.430
Many folks are comfortable
running Envoy themselves,

00:12:54.430 --> 00:12:57.040
particularly
sophisticated users who

00:12:57.040 --> 00:12:59.820
need to use it in very
custom applications,

00:12:59.820 --> 00:13:01.930
and Istio as well.

00:13:01.930 --> 00:13:05.330
Other folks would prefer
that their Envoys and Istio

00:13:05.330 --> 00:13:09.370
service mesh control planes
be managed because these are--

00:13:09.370 --> 00:13:15.120
and I think this really leads
into Traffic Director, which

00:13:15.120 --> 00:13:19.360
is Google's managed control
plane for both Envoy and Istio.

00:13:19.360 --> 00:13:23.418
So my colleague Arun is going
to talk a bit more about Traffic

00:13:23.418 --> 00:13:23.960
Director now.

00:13:23.960 --> 00:13:25.650
So I'll hand it over to him.

00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:34.140
ARUNKUMAR JAYARAMAN:
Thanks, Harvey.

00:13:34.140 --> 00:13:37.260
I'm Arum, a software
engineer at Google.

00:13:37.260 --> 00:13:40.400
So Harvey talked about--

00:13:40.400 --> 00:13:42.570
Harvey talked about
Envoy, and data plane,

00:13:42.570 --> 00:13:46.490
and xDS API as universal
data plane API.

00:13:46.490 --> 00:13:50.600
The xDS API is often coupled
with the sophisticated control

00:13:50.600 --> 00:13:54.170
plane to drastically reduce
the operational complexity

00:13:54.170 --> 00:13:55.910
of deployments.

00:13:55.910 --> 00:14:00.110
In that context, I would like to
introduce Traffic Doctor, which

00:14:00.110 --> 00:14:05.090
is a globally-deployed, fully
GCP-managed xDS server control

00:14:05.090 --> 00:14:08.630
plane to serve xDS-compliant
planes like Envoy.

00:14:11.800 --> 00:14:14.470
Traffic Director SOs
and network policies

00:14:14.470 --> 00:14:17.970
and globally load balanced
and centrally held

00:14:17.970 --> 00:14:23.180
check end points to the
xDS client via the xDS API.

00:14:23.180 --> 00:14:26.060
For global load balancing,
Traffic Director

00:14:26.060 --> 00:14:31.790
has a full view of the load
originating from every GCP zone

00:14:31.790 --> 00:14:34.340
and the capacity
available in every GCP

00:14:34.340 --> 00:14:37.250
zone for the service deployed.

00:14:37.250 --> 00:14:39.890
And with that knowledge
and information,

00:14:39.890 --> 00:14:42.740
Traffic Director has the
ability to route lines

00:14:42.740 --> 00:14:46.610
to the closest
zones with capacity,

00:14:46.610 --> 00:14:51.490
and the proximity is measured
based on network latencies.

00:14:51.490 --> 00:14:54.040
And when the region is
at capacity, or if there

00:14:54.040 --> 00:14:55.930
are failures in the
region, Traffic Director

00:14:55.930 --> 00:14:59.830
has the ability to route
clients to the closest

00:14:59.830 --> 00:15:04.640
zones in the adjacent
regions, or nearby regions.

00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:06.760
The load information and
the capacity information

00:15:06.760 --> 00:15:10.330
in Traffic Director is fed
into a autoscaler as well,

00:15:10.330 --> 00:15:15.160
so this allows autoscaler
to do a traffic

00:15:15.160 --> 00:15:17.140
demand driven autoscaling.

00:15:17.140 --> 00:15:20.250
And this autoscaler
scales up the instances.

00:15:20.250 --> 00:15:22.090
And Traffic Director,
in the interim,

00:15:22.090 --> 00:15:26.200
has the ability to route
client request to nearby zones,

00:15:26.200 --> 00:15:29.270
thereby efficiently serving the
service while the autoscaler

00:15:29.270 --> 00:15:31.990
scales up the instances.

00:15:31.990 --> 00:15:35.020
Traffic Director also performs
centralized health checking.

00:15:35.020 --> 00:15:38.200
So this eliminates the need
for every client in the network

00:15:38.200 --> 00:15:40.150
to health check
every other back-end,

00:15:40.150 --> 00:15:42.790
so we call it as
NSquare health checking.

00:15:42.790 --> 00:15:45.430
And this could be a significant
source of network traffic

00:15:45.430 --> 00:15:47.150
in your deployment.

00:15:47.150 --> 00:15:51.340
Finally, the xDS API is
observed by Traffic Director

00:15:51.340 --> 00:15:52.443
via Google front-end.

00:15:52.443 --> 00:15:53.860
This is the same
front-end that is

00:15:53.860 --> 00:15:59.110
used to serve other major
Google services, like Gmail,

00:15:59.110 --> 00:16:00.440
such and such.

00:16:00.440 --> 00:16:02.740
So the Google front-end
has the intelligence

00:16:02.740 --> 00:16:07.630
to route the xDS client
request to any Traffic Director

00:16:07.630 --> 00:16:09.610
instance globally
that has capacity.

00:16:09.610 --> 00:16:11.860
And independent of which
Traffic Director instance

00:16:11.860 --> 00:16:13.943
serves the request,
the load balancing,

00:16:13.943 --> 00:16:15.610
the global load
balancing for the client

00:16:15.610 --> 00:16:19.520
is always based on
the client's zone.

00:16:19.520 --> 00:16:21.950
So I want to dive
a little bit deeper

00:16:21.950 --> 00:16:25.970
into the API model for
the Traffic Director.

00:16:25.970 --> 00:16:30.530
The GCP data model is
the same as HTTPS LB.

00:16:30.530 --> 00:16:32.360
In fact, under the
hood, Traffic Director

00:16:32.360 --> 00:16:35.500
uses the same
infrastructure as HTTPS LB

00:16:35.500 --> 00:16:39.320
for global load balancing and
centralized health checking.

00:16:39.320 --> 00:16:42.590
So we have a new load
balancing scheme called

00:16:42.590 --> 00:16:47.720
internal self managed that helps
direct a configuration targeted

00:16:47.720 --> 00:16:49.610
for Traffic Director.

00:16:49.610 --> 00:16:52.130
The global forwarding
rule can, in the context

00:16:52.130 --> 00:16:53.720
of Traffic Director,
can be thought

00:16:53.720 --> 00:16:58.010
of as a reference to a
configuration for one entity.

00:16:58.010 --> 00:17:02.030
For example, it may refer to a
configuration of a Kubernetes

00:17:02.030 --> 00:17:02.720
service.

00:17:02.720 --> 00:17:06.290
And if the user wants
to create a mesh, that

00:17:06.290 --> 00:17:08.390
is a combination of
multiple configurations,

00:17:08.390 --> 00:17:11.960
the user can do that by
combining multiple forwarding

00:17:11.960 --> 00:17:14.460
rule configs with the
same VPC network name.

00:17:14.460 --> 00:17:16.490
So in some sense,
the VPC network name

00:17:16.490 --> 00:17:20.630
acts as a logical identifier
for the mesh name.

00:17:20.630 --> 00:17:24.890
The URL map has the request
routing, the rules and action,

00:17:24.890 --> 00:17:26.839
pointing to the
service represented

00:17:26.839 --> 00:17:28.730
by the back-end
service data model.

00:17:28.730 --> 00:17:31.580
The back-ends are added
to the back-end service.

00:17:31.580 --> 00:17:35.990
The back-ends could either be
a VM represented by an instance

00:17:35.990 --> 00:17:39.560
group or data model, or
it could be the container

00:17:39.560 --> 00:17:43.670
IP ports represented by a data
model called network end point

00:17:43.670 --> 00:17:44.740
groups.

00:17:44.740 --> 00:17:48.370
In orchestrated deployments,
like Kubernetes and GKE,

00:17:48.370 --> 00:17:52.430
as the end points get scheduled,
as the ports get scheduled,

00:17:52.430 --> 00:17:54.110
the network end point--

00:17:54.110 --> 00:17:56.690
a controller called
net controller

00:17:56.690 --> 00:17:59.960
is responsible for keeping
the network end point group

00:17:59.960 --> 00:18:02.270
updated, so that the
back-end service has

00:18:02.270 --> 00:18:05.160
the proper reflection off end
points to service mapping.

00:18:05.160 --> 00:18:08.360
So this allows Traffic Director
to homogeneously manage

00:18:08.360 --> 00:18:13.710
the VM and the
container deployments.

00:18:13.710 --> 00:18:16.600
So far we've talked about the
Traffic Director beta features.

00:18:16.600 --> 00:18:20.580
So now I would like to introduce
Traffic Director's traffic

00:18:20.580 --> 00:18:24.060
control features that are alpha.

00:18:24.060 --> 00:18:27.210
Traffic control is
a set of features

00:18:27.210 --> 00:18:32.390
that helps you control the flow
of traffic in your deployment.

00:18:35.120 --> 00:18:39.180
It allows you to
specify matching rules

00:18:39.180 --> 00:18:41.400
for your incoming
request, and allows

00:18:41.400 --> 00:18:45.960
you to set up actions
for the matched request.

00:18:45.960 --> 00:18:48.970
And then at a service
level, per service,

00:18:48.970 --> 00:18:51.840
it allows you to set
traffic policies that

00:18:51.840 --> 00:18:53.820
helps in traffic
shipping and traffic

00:18:53.820 --> 00:18:57.480
routing the incoming request
to your target destination

00:18:57.480 --> 00:18:58.930
service.

00:18:58.930 --> 00:19:02.010
Not necessarily related
to traffic control,

00:19:02.010 --> 00:19:04.470
but as the number of
configurations grow,

00:19:04.470 --> 00:19:09.390
there may need to test your
configuration through a test

00:19:09.390 --> 00:19:12.000
pool of [INAUDIBLE],,
or perhaps your mesh

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:14.880
has [INAUDIBLE] of
different config scope.

00:19:14.880 --> 00:19:17.130
And in such cases,
you can potentially

00:19:17.130 --> 00:19:19.320
use a configuration
filtering option

00:19:19.320 --> 00:19:21.450
where you can target
the configuration

00:19:21.450 --> 00:19:23.670
to a subset of
[INAUDIBLE] identified

00:19:23.670 --> 00:19:27.340
through their metadata.

00:19:27.340 --> 00:19:31.680
So let me dive a little deeper
into each of these features.

00:19:31.680 --> 00:19:35.840
For request matching
for a set of rules

00:19:35.840 --> 00:19:39.860
to specify on what to match
for the incoming request,

00:19:39.860 --> 00:19:44.900
you can set up a host match,
you can set up a path match.

00:19:44.900 --> 00:19:47.270
And there are variants like
prefix match, suffix match,

00:19:47.270 --> 00:19:49.550
and regular
expressions and so on.

00:19:49.550 --> 00:19:53.540
Or you can set up matches based
on anything in your header,

00:19:53.540 --> 00:19:56.670
like in cookies, user
agents and so on.

00:19:56.670 --> 00:20:01.310
And, once a match is found,
you can perform certain actions

00:20:01.310 --> 00:20:02.360
on the match request.

00:20:02.360 --> 00:20:05.570
The standard actions are
redirects, rewrites, header

00:20:05.570 --> 00:20:07.850
transforms, or
sending the traffic

00:20:07.850 --> 00:20:09.290
to a particular service.

00:20:09.290 --> 00:20:11.930
But I want to talk about
a few interesting use

00:20:11.930 --> 00:20:14.010
cases and the actions.

00:20:14.010 --> 00:20:17.090
So let's say you have a binary
that you want to canary,

00:20:17.090 --> 00:20:23.660
or you have a monolithic binary
and you are starting to have--

00:20:23.660 --> 00:20:26.600
modernize your application by
containerizing it and going

00:20:26.600 --> 00:20:28.400
to a microservice model.

00:20:28.400 --> 00:20:30.680
You can use an action--

00:20:30.680 --> 00:20:35.360
or traffic action called traffic
splitting, where you can set up

00:20:35.360 --> 00:20:38.030
a small percentage of traffic
to go to your new deployment,

00:20:38.030 --> 00:20:39.980
or the new binary,
and over time,

00:20:39.980 --> 00:20:42.650
as you qualify the binary,
as you get more confidence,

00:20:42.650 --> 00:20:45.620
you can send more and more
traffic to the new binary,

00:20:45.620 --> 00:20:49.400
enabling a smooth transition.

00:20:49.400 --> 00:20:52.100
So let's say you
want to understand

00:20:52.100 --> 00:20:54.310
the resiliency of
your microservice,

00:20:54.310 --> 00:20:56.140
then one of your
core dependencies

00:20:56.140 --> 00:20:58.790
starts responding with errors.

00:20:58.790 --> 00:21:02.710
You can set up an action
called fault injection

00:21:02.710 --> 00:21:06.610
where you can specify
thresholds and type of errors

00:21:06.610 --> 00:21:10.000
that your service
would respond with.

00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:12.380
And using that,
you can figure out

00:21:12.380 --> 00:21:16.380
how your deployment behaves in
the presence of those errors.

00:21:16.380 --> 00:21:21.370
So even at Google, we have needs
where we have test [INAUDIBLE]

00:21:21.370 --> 00:21:21.970
service.

00:21:21.970 --> 00:21:24.970
And instead of using synthetic
or simulated traffic,

00:21:24.970 --> 00:21:28.030
sometimes there is a need
to have production traffic

00:21:28.030 --> 00:21:30.400
and test out your test binaries.

00:21:30.400 --> 00:21:33.340
To do that, you can do something
called traffic mirroring, where

00:21:33.340 --> 00:21:38.050
you can use the traffic control
features to setup traffic

00:21:38.050 --> 00:21:40.630
to be mirrored to
a shadow service

00:21:40.630 --> 00:21:43.870
without impacting your
production deployments,

00:21:43.870 --> 00:21:46.870
and the shadow service could be
your test service and your test

00:21:46.870 --> 00:21:49.970
bed helping to qualify your
test binary with the production

00:21:49.970 --> 00:21:50.470
traffic.

00:21:54.050 --> 00:21:56.750
So we talked about Traffic
Director's global load

00:21:56.750 --> 00:21:57.500
balancing.

00:21:57.500 --> 00:22:00.800
So the global load balancing
helps pick out your zone

00:22:00.800 --> 00:22:02.850
with capacity.

00:22:02.850 --> 00:22:06.050
However, there may
be needs for the user

00:22:06.050 --> 00:22:09.890
to set up more fine grained load
balancing schemes, so we allow

00:22:09.890 --> 00:22:14.240
a per service load balancing
configuration that influences

00:22:14.240 --> 00:22:17.360
how a back-end is
picked within a zone,

00:22:17.360 --> 00:22:20.750
while the global load
balancing picks out

00:22:20.750 --> 00:22:22.060
the zone with capacity.

00:22:22.060 --> 00:22:24.620
So this two-tiered
load balancing

00:22:24.620 --> 00:22:30.200
enables you to have options like
in a round robin or beta round

00:22:30.200 --> 00:22:35.120
robin, or affinity and such, and
fine tune your load balancing

00:22:35.120 --> 00:22:38.053
schemes while still leveraging
the global load balancing

00:22:38.053 --> 00:22:39.220
offered by Traffic Director.

00:22:42.630 --> 00:22:46.080
So onto the final slide.

00:22:46.080 --> 00:22:47.770
So as a service
owner, if you would

00:22:47.770 --> 00:22:53.230
want to have a good control
of the volume of connections

00:22:53.230 --> 00:22:55.570
open from a client
to your service,

00:22:55.570 --> 00:23:00.040
you can set up something
called circuit breakers, which

00:23:00.040 --> 00:23:02.110
is a way to apply back
pressure on the client

00:23:02.110 --> 00:23:05.140
and have the clients fail
fast rather than overwhelm

00:23:05.140 --> 00:23:06.020
your clients--

00:23:06.020 --> 00:23:09.800
or sorry, overwhelm
your back-ends.

00:23:09.800 --> 00:23:11.920
Oftentimes, it
takes a small amount

00:23:11.920 --> 00:23:15.250
of time for the control
plane to turn around

00:23:15.250 --> 00:23:17.360
with a globally
optimal decision.

00:23:17.360 --> 00:23:20.710
However, if you want a better
resiliency in your data plane,

00:23:20.710 --> 00:23:23.680
where if a particular
back-end instance goes down,

00:23:23.680 --> 00:23:27.490
or a VM goes down, and if you
want to respond to that quickly

00:23:27.490 --> 00:23:29.680
before the control
plane can detect

00:23:29.680 --> 00:23:33.477
the state of-- our change in the
health state of the back-ends,

00:23:33.477 --> 00:23:35.560
you can set up something
called outlier detection,

00:23:35.560 --> 00:23:40.330
where in the data
plane you identify

00:23:40.330 --> 00:23:42.850
unresponsive back-ends, and
[INAUDIBLE] those back-ends

00:23:42.850 --> 00:23:44.800
until the control
plane turns around

00:23:44.800 --> 00:23:47.110
with a globally
optimal decision.

00:23:50.170 --> 00:23:52.660
So with that, I'm going to
pass the presentation over

00:23:52.660 --> 00:23:56.170
to Larry who's going to talk
about an interesting mesh use

00:23:56.170 --> 00:24:00.608
case and modernization
of telco infrastructure.

00:24:00.608 --> 00:24:01.650
LARRY PETERSON: I'm good.

00:24:01.650 --> 00:24:02.896
Thank you.

00:24:02.896 --> 00:24:06.207
[APPLAUSE]

00:24:07.630 --> 00:24:09.490
So now that you
understand service meshes,

00:24:09.490 --> 00:24:12.840
I'm going to break it,
or at least challenge it

00:24:12.840 --> 00:24:15.508
with a different use case,
which is the telco use case.

00:24:15.508 --> 00:24:17.550
So you need to have a
picture in your head, which

00:24:17.550 --> 00:24:19.660
is that it's a multi-cloud
world, and, of course,

00:24:19.660 --> 00:24:22.290
we know there are a lot of
data center public clouds.

00:24:22.290 --> 00:24:27.640
You probably also know that
there are intermediate clouds.

00:24:27.640 --> 00:24:29.040
They live within the telcos.

00:24:29.040 --> 00:24:30.780
They live in exchange points.

00:24:30.780 --> 00:24:32.520
And the current push
is, I'm sure you're

00:24:32.520 --> 00:24:35.770
aware if you've been following
the hype, is towards the edge.

00:24:35.770 --> 00:24:37.620
So the question
is, what is exactly

00:24:37.620 --> 00:24:39.602
going to happen here as
we get out to the edge?

00:24:39.602 --> 00:24:41.310
And how are we going
to do service meshes

00:24:41.310 --> 00:24:43.000
across this entire thing?

00:24:43.000 --> 00:24:45.870
Now, this particular picture
is a little bit aspirational

00:24:45.870 --> 00:24:48.450
because it shows a
really nice clean edge.

00:24:48.450 --> 00:24:50.250
The truth of the
matter is there's

00:24:50.250 --> 00:24:52.208
a lot of legacy hardware
at the edge right now.

00:24:52.208 --> 00:24:54.750
Now, of course, it depends on
whose edge we're talking about,

00:24:54.750 --> 00:24:57.120
but I'm going to talk about
the telco edge for a minute.

00:24:57.120 --> 00:24:59.037
So these are the central
offices of the telco,

00:24:59.037 --> 00:25:00.745
it's the head-ins of
the cable companies.

00:25:00.745 --> 00:25:02.829
And if you were ever to
go inside of one of those,

00:25:02.829 --> 00:25:04.650
you'd think you had
walked into a museum

00:25:04.650 --> 00:25:06.240
because it's full
of hardware that

00:25:06.240 --> 00:25:07.440
goes back many, many years.

00:25:07.440 --> 00:25:10.710
As long as it's generating
money, they keep it running.

00:25:10.710 --> 00:25:12.750
You might find 300
different kinds

00:25:12.750 --> 00:25:15.870
of hardware devices inside
of a telco central office.

00:25:15.870 --> 00:25:18.290
And so it's clearly in
the telco's best interest

00:25:18.290 --> 00:25:20.437
to try to modernize
that infrastructure.

00:25:20.437 --> 00:25:22.020
And what they've
been doing is they've

00:25:22.020 --> 00:25:25.410
been trying to commoditize,
virtualize, and disaggregate

00:25:25.410 --> 00:25:27.000
the access network.

00:25:27.000 --> 00:25:28.470
And by access
network, I'm talking

00:25:28.470 --> 00:25:31.080
about the cellular
network and the fiber

00:25:31.080 --> 00:25:33.420
to the home, the passive
optical networks.

00:25:33.420 --> 00:25:34.920
And so they're
working hard at this.

00:25:34.920 --> 00:25:37.230
They basically are trying
to adopt the same technology

00:25:37.230 --> 00:25:39.063
that's back in the
public cloud and apply it

00:25:39.063 --> 00:25:42.180
to these edges full of
these specialized devices.

00:25:42.180 --> 00:25:43.950
There's a project at
the ONF that we're

00:25:43.950 --> 00:25:47.650
involved with called CORD,
which is an umbrella,

00:25:47.650 --> 00:25:50.110
but it also is a
particular implementation,

00:25:50.110 --> 00:25:52.000
which is an acronym
for central office

00:25:52.000 --> 00:25:53.890
we architected as a data center.

00:25:53.890 --> 00:25:57.900
But the idea is to take
those commodity virtualized

00:25:57.900 --> 00:26:00.660
type technologies and
bring them to the edge.

00:26:00.660 --> 00:26:02.628
And so that's well in progress.

00:26:02.628 --> 00:26:04.170
A lot of disaggregation
has happened.

00:26:04.170 --> 00:26:06.390
It's being put back
together again.

00:26:06.390 --> 00:26:08.345
And that does, in fact,
get us to the point

00:26:08.345 --> 00:26:09.720
that I'm going to
call this thing

00:26:09.720 --> 00:26:12.360
the Access-Edge, because
it's a point where

00:26:12.360 --> 00:26:15.720
traditional conventional
cloud technology and access

00:26:15.720 --> 00:26:17.568
technology coexists.

00:26:17.568 --> 00:26:18.360
And so that's the--

00:26:18.360 --> 00:26:19.830
this is called the Access-Edge.

00:26:19.830 --> 00:26:24.220
Now, of course, from
a telco point of view,

00:26:24.220 --> 00:26:26.730
they are interested in offering
services to subscribers.

00:26:26.730 --> 00:26:29.220
So we have to think at a little
bit finer granularity every

00:26:29.220 --> 00:26:32.010
now and then in terms, not of
a service mesh but as a service

00:26:32.010 --> 00:26:32.880
chain.

00:26:32.880 --> 00:26:35.010
And in the near future,
that service chain

00:26:35.010 --> 00:26:37.500
is going to be distributed
across the Access-Edge,

00:26:37.500 --> 00:26:40.920
some intermediate telco clouds,
or internet exchange points,

00:26:40.920 --> 00:26:43.620
all the way back up
into the data center.

00:26:43.620 --> 00:26:46.240
And it will, in fact, go
onto the premises as well.

00:26:46.240 --> 00:26:50.340
So there will be an on-premises
aspect to the edges in addition

00:26:50.340 --> 00:26:52.332
to the Access-Edge.

00:26:52.332 --> 00:26:54.540
But one of the unique things
about the access network

00:26:54.540 --> 00:26:56.590
is that it also
supports mobility.

00:26:56.590 --> 00:26:58.290
And so these service
chains need to be

00:26:58.290 --> 00:27:01.050
able to migrate from one
edge to another, which,

00:27:01.050 --> 00:27:03.120
as you move functionality
closer to the edges,

00:27:03.120 --> 00:27:05.360
it means that that functionality
has to move as well.

00:27:05.360 --> 00:27:07.110
So if you pull back
for a second and think

00:27:07.110 --> 00:27:12.530
about the earlier generations
of the cellular network 2G, 4G--

00:27:12.530 --> 00:27:16.495
to 4G, it's basically mobility
of the broadband access.

00:27:16.495 --> 00:27:18.870
But now if we're going to move
functionality to the edge,

00:27:18.870 --> 00:27:21.000
we have to move the
functions as well.

00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:23.448
That doesn't mean VM
migration, but somehow I

00:27:23.448 --> 00:27:25.740
have to support the fact that
you had something running

00:27:25.740 --> 00:27:27.600
on your behalf at one
edge and it may now

00:27:27.600 --> 00:27:29.070
need to run another edge.

00:27:29.070 --> 00:27:31.920
So, in effect, the cloud
becomes mobile as well as

00:27:31.920 --> 00:27:34.810
the broadband connectivity.

00:27:34.810 --> 00:27:37.230
So this is the big picture
of what the telco world is

00:27:37.230 --> 00:27:42.630
after, and that is to be
part of this end-to-end cloud

00:27:42.630 --> 00:27:44.710
environment.

00:27:44.710 --> 00:27:47.252
So they had to start
from the beginning.

00:27:47.252 --> 00:27:48.960
You all know microservices
because you've

00:27:48.960 --> 00:27:50.940
been breaking your
monolithic applications down

00:27:50.940 --> 00:27:52.110
for a long time.

00:27:52.110 --> 00:27:54.342
The telco industry is just
now trying to do that.

00:27:54.342 --> 00:27:56.550
So I wanted to spend just
a couple of minutes talking

00:27:56.550 --> 00:27:58.860
about that, because it's
after you break it down

00:27:58.860 --> 00:28:02.350
that you get to put it back
together in a service mesh.

00:28:02.350 --> 00:28:05.850
Well, like I said,
these central offices

00:28:05.850 --> 00:28:10.240
are full of proprietary
closed bundled hardware.

00:28:10.240 --> 00:28:11.988
And it's another alphabet soup.

00:28:11.988 --> 00:28:13.780
And if you know any of
these, good for you.

00:28:13.780 --> 00:28:15.360
But in the access
networks, we've

00:28:15.360 --> 00:28:20.100
got PONs and RANs and
eNodeBs and BNGs and so on.

00:28:20.100 --> 00:28:22.380
Not important what
those all are.

00:28:22.380 --> 00:28:23.950
It's the various boxes--

00:28:23.950 --> 00:28:27.810
and again, they're monolithic
closed pieces of hardware,

00:28:27.810 --> 00:28:29.280
and they're being disaggregated.

00:28:29.280 --> 00:28:30.840
And so part of
that disaggregation

00:28:30.840 --> 00:28:32.923
is to split the control
plane from the data plane.

00:28:32.923 --> 00:28:34.920
This is standard SDN practices.

00:28:34.920 --> 00:28:37.290
Part of it is to break the
monolithic pieces of code

00:28:37.290 --> 00:28:38.370
into microservices.

00:28:38.370 --> 00:28:40.983
So that's what the rest of
the cloud industry is doing.

00:28:40.983 --> 00:28:42.900
But the other thing
that's kind of interesting

00:28:42.900 --> 00:28:44.510
here is, because,
first and foremost,

00:28:44.510 --> 00:28:46.260
there are communications
services running,

00:28:46.260 --> 00:28:48.390
sometimes you want to
take those microservices

00:28:48.390 --> 00:28:50.850
and drop them down into
the switching fabric.

00:28:50.850 --> 00:28:54.840
And so because the data plane
in modern white box switches

00:28:54.840 --> 00:28:56.910
is also programmable,
I can take what

00:28:56.910 --> 00:28:58.890
used to run in a
microservice and move it down

00:28:58.890 --> 00:29:00.180
into the forwarding plane.

00:29:00.180 --> 00:29:02.670
Now we're talking
hardware forwarding plane.

00:29:02.670 --> 00:29:06.030
So these are all aspects
of disaggregation.

00:29:06.030 --> 00:29:08.010
It's not just
microservices, it's

00:29:08.010 --> 00:29:12.190
programming the forwarding
plane in the switches as well.

00:29:12.190 --> 00:29:15.370
Well, let me try to make
it slightly more concrete.

00:29:15.370 --> 00:29:17.508
So this is now a service mesh.

00:29:17.508 --> 00:29:19.800
Without going through the
details of how you break down

00:29:19.800 --> 00:29:21.510
any particular bit
of hardware, this

00:29:21.510 --> 00:29:23.770
is a service mesh for
the cellular use case.

00:29:23.770 --> 00:29:25.500
And, again, there's
a bunch of acronyms.

00:29:25.500 --> 00:29:27.632
What's important about
the color coding here

00:29:27.632 --> 00:29:30.090
is that everything in blue is
a function that's implemented

00:29:30.090 --> 00:29:32.100
in the switching fabric,
everything that's red

00:29:32.100 --> 00:29:34.230
is a function that's
implemented in a container,

00:29:34.230 --> 00:29:36.070
in a microservice.

00:29:36.070 --> 00:29:38.310
The solid lines are data paths.

00:29:38.310 --> 00:29:41.070
Packets actually flow over
on behalf of end subscribers.

00:29:41.070 --> 00:29:46.440
The dotted lines
are control paths.

00:29:46.440 --> 00:29:48.960
And this is clearly
an opportunity

00:29:48.960 --> 00:29:52.870
to bring traditional service
mesh technology to bear,

00:29:52.870 --> 00:29:56.240
so we could run Onvoy on the
video archive and the CDN

00:29:56.240 --> 00:29:57.630
out at the edge.

00:29:57.630 --> 00:30:00.300
The other thing to note is
that this is, in fact, also,

00:30:00.300 --> 00:30:01.380
multi-cloud.

00:30:01.380 --> 00:30:02.817
So bits and pieces of it.

00:30:02.817 --> 00:30:04.650
And they can be configured
in different ways

00:30:04.650 --> 00:30:05.525
with bits and pieces.

00:30:05.525 --> 00:30:08.850
But here, will, in fact,
run at the Access-Edge,

00:30:08.850 --> 00:30:12.858
and pieces of it will run back
in, perhaps, a commodity cloud.

00:30:12.858 --> 00:30:14.400
So this is a
configuration that we've

00:30:14.400 --> 00:30:17.880
been working on in conjunction
with the service mesh

00:30:17.880 --> 00:30:20.490
world and the ONF to
try to demonstrate

00:30:20.490 --> 00:30:21.930
this in the cellular case.

00:30:24.820 --> 00:30:27.970
And by the way, this is just
representative in the sense

00:30:27.970 --> 00:30:30.603
that we assume there will
be other edge services.

00:30:30.603 --> 00:30:32.020
And by the way,
those services are

00:30:32.020 --> 00:30:34.240
the ones that need high
bandwidth, low latency,

00:30:34.240 --> 00:30:36.670
and deal with a scalable
number of internet of things,

00:30:36.670 --> 00:30:39.378
or whatever autonomous vehicles
are out there at the edge,

00:30:39.378 --> 00:30:41.920
and there will, of course, be
multiple cloud services running

00:30:41.920 --> 00:30:43.253
back in the data center as well.

00:30:45.870 --> 00:30:47.677
So we've blown these
things into bits.

00:30:47.677 --> 00:30:49.510
Now we have to put them
back together again.

00:30:49.510 --> 00:30:51.750
And this is where something
like a service mesh

00:30:51.750 --> 00:30:54.300
is an important tool.

00:30:54.300 --> 00:30:56.490
But operationalizing
this disaggregated system

00:30:56.490 --> 00:30:59.460
is a little bit different than
simply connecting together

00:30:59.460 --> 00:31:01.170
a bunch of microservices,
because there

00:31:01.170 --> 00:31:02.295
are other elements in here.

00:31:02.295 --> 00:31:05.963
So I wanted to walk through
that for just a few minutes.

00:31:05.963 --> 00:31:07.880
So I think of this as
the disruptor's dilemma.

00:31:07.880 --> 00:31:09.630
And this is, in fact,
the critical problem

00:31:09.630 --> 00:31:11.888
that the telco industry
is facing right now.

00:31:11.888 --> 00:31:14.180
On the one hand, they wanted
to disaggregate everything

00:31:14.180 --> 00:31:15.590
because that's what
spurs innovation.

00:31:15.590 --> 00:31:16.710
I've got smaller pieces.

00:31:16.710 --> 00:31:19.905
It's not tightly bundled and
controlled by the vendors.

00:31:19.905 --> 00:31:21.530
That is, in fact,
the value proposition

00:31:21.530 --> 00:31:23.402
of open networking and SDN.

00:31:23.402 --> 00:31:24.860
On the other hand,
you can't deploy

00:31:24.860 --> 00:31:26.360
a bunch of disaggregated
components,

00:31:26.360 --> 00:31:29.090
you have to integrate
them back together again.

00:31:29.090 --> 00:31:31.610
Now, in the telco world, because
what they're used to doing

00:31:31.610 --> 00:31:34.070
is dropping in these
bundled solutions,

00:31:34.070 --> 00:31:36.140
they have a certain
mindset as to how

00:31:36.140 --> 00:31:37.970
that integration happens.

00:31:37.970 --> 00:31:40.028
Basically, they take the
disaggregated components

00:31:40.028 --> 00:31:41.570
and they repackage
them until they're

00:31:41.570 --> 00:31:43.098
a monolithic solution again.

00:31:43.098 --> 00:31:45.140
And then that's what
they're prepared to drop in.

00:31:45.140 --> 00:31:46.682
But that kind of
defeats the purpose.

00:31:46.682 --> 00:31:48.920
You're not doing
continuous integration

00:31:48.920 --> 00:31:51.620
of the individual
components if you fall back

00:31:51.620 --> 00:31:54.500
to that kind of hard
coded integration.

00:31:54.500 --> 00:31:59.900
And so the key focus here
is that they also automate

00:31:59.900 --> 00:32:02.130
the integration process.

00:32:02.130 --> 00:32:04.510
And so that involves
a declarative intent.

00:32:04.510 --> 00:32:07.010
I basically-- and this is very
much in the spirit of service

00:32:07.010 --> 00:32:07.510
meshes--

00:32:07.510 --> 00:32:12.350
I want to lift the operational
parameters out of the services

00:32:12.350 --> 00:32:14.930
and up into a service
control plane leaving

00:32:14.930 --> 00:32:18.290
behind the implementation
of the service proper.

00:32:18.290 --> 00:32:20.120
So I separate the
development concerns

00:32:20.120 --> 00:32:22.190
for the operational
deployment concerns.

00:32:22.190 --> 00:32:25.340
I want to avoid those hard coded
dependencies between the pieces

00:32:25.340 --> 00:32:27.070
that I've just carved off.

00:32:27.070 --> 00:32:30.260
Second and consistent with that,
I want a centralized authority.

00:32:30.260 --> 00:32:32.953
I don't want to go touch 80
pieces of code every time

00:32:32.953 --> 00:32:33.620
I make a change.

00:32:33.620 --> 00:32:36.810
I want to centrally
declare this is my intent,

00:32:36.810 --> 00:32:38.720
and then make it
so everywhere else.

00:32:38.720 --> 00:32:42.090
Otherwise, I'm going to end
up-- instead of managing 300,

00:32:42.090 --> 00:32:45.620
I'm going to be managing
30,000 different microservices

00:32:45.620 --> 00:32:49.580
and disaggregated components
in the central offices.

00:32:49.580 --> 00:32:51.290
That's not a good place to be.

00:32:51.290 --> 00:32:55.250
The third point is we have to
be implementation agnostic.

00:32:55.250 --> 00:32:57.555
And this is really
critical, because it's not

00:32:57.555 --> 00:32:58.930
just microservices,
there's going

00:32:58.930 --> 00:33:02.630
to be functionality implemented
in Brownfield hardware

00:33:02.630 --> 00:33:04.340
devices for some
time, there are going

00:33:04.340 --> 00:33:06.800
to be functions implemented
in virtual machines,

00:33:06.800 --> 00:33:08.300
there are going to
be microservices,

00:33:08.300 --> 00:33:10.790
and there's going to be
functions that are implemented

00:33:10.790 --> 00:33:11.930
in the switching fabric.

00:33:11.930 --> 00:33:14.780
And so we can't bake assumptions
into our service control plane

00:33:14.780 --> 00:33:17.460
about where things
are implemented.

00:33:17.460 --> 00:33:19.040
So the solution
that we have come

00:33:19.040 --> 00:33:22.160
to as of now, and
what we're deploying

00:33:22.160 --> 00:33:25.250
in trials within the
major carriers that

00:33:25.250 --> 00:33:27.500
are partners of the ONF--

00:33:27.500 --> 00:33:29.660
just two words about the ONF.

00:33:29.660 --> 00:33:32.540
Our partners are our
network operators,

00:33:32.540 --> 00:33:34.010
and that's a broadly
defined term.

00:33:34.010 --> 00:33:37.310
Google's a member, AT&amp;T,
Comcast, Deutsche Telekom

00:33:37.310 --> 00:33:37.890
and so on.

00:33:37.890 --> 00:33:39.830
So are the ones that
want to see this happen,

00:33:39.830 --> 00:33:41.580
and the set of vendors
that are supporting

00:33:41.580 --> 00:33:43.230
them to try to make it happen.

00:33:43.230 --> 00:33:46.470
But in that setting, we are
putting together solutions,

00:33:46.470 --> 00:33:49.340
and we have a service control
plane that's called XOS.

00:33:49.340 --> 00:33:51.230
And the important thing
to take away from it

00:33:51.230 --> 00:33:54.230
is that it's basically managing
a service data plane that

00:33:54.230 --> 00:33:57.740
has a collection of different
disaggregated components

00:33:57.740 --> 00:33:59.330
that it's managing,
some of those

00:33:59.330 --> 00:34:01.880
are legacy virtual network
functions running in VMs,

00:34:01.880 --> 00:34:04.310
some of them are horizontally
scalable microservices,

00:34:04.310 --> 00:34:06.770
and some of them are
SDN control apps,

00:34:06.770 --> 00:34:08.670
and others are possible as well.

00:34:08.670 --> 00:34:12.630
They could have been
legacy SAS for that matter.

00:34:12.630 --> 00:34:15.630
And in the very
declarative way, you tell,

00:34:15.630 --> 00:34:17.991
you give XOS a schema
that says, this

00:34:17.991 --> 00:34:19.949
is how I want all of
these parts glued together

00:34:19.949 --> 00:34:22.502
in a logical space, make it
so done in the data plane.

00:34:22.502 --> 00:34:24.210
So it's a very consistent
model with what

00:34:24.210 --> 00:34:27.610
happens with service meshes.

00:34:27.610 --> 00:34:29.701
Now, having-- pause it at that.

00:34:29.701 --> 00:34:30.909
Let's step back for a second.

00:34:30.909 --> 00:34:33.790
And hopefully you recognize
the parallels here.

00:34:33.790 --> 00:34:36.080
It's the same story,
different level.

00:34:36.080 --> 00:34:38.949
And so the parallels
are at L7, we

00:34:38.949 --> 00:34:40.389
have a programmable data plane.

00:34:40.389 --> 00:34:42.040
It's programmed in proxies.

00:34:42.040 --> 00:34:46.420
In L2, L3, it's programming
A6 in the switches.

00:34:46.420 --> 00:34:49.750
We have an API, it's
xDS in the L7 case.

00:34:49.750 --> 00:34:52.989
It's either open
flow or P4 runtime,

00:34:52.989 --> 00:34:56.280
both happen in the CORD case.

00:34:56.280 --> 00:34:58.990
In the control plane, we
have something like Istio.

00:34:58.990 --> 00:35:01.082
In the CORD case,
we have something

00:35:01.082 --> 00:35:03.290
like ONOS, which is an open
network operating system.

00:35:03.290 --> 00:35:05.680
So think of whatever your
favorite SDN controller is

00:35:05.680 --> 00:35:07.095
and substitute it there.

00:35:07.095 --> 00:35:10.730
By the way, you'll
notice I left out L4.

00:35:10.730 --> 00:35:12.628
L4 could be solved in
either one of those.

00:35:12.628 --> 00:35:14.170
I didn't want to
put it in either one

00:35:14.170 --> 00:35:18.658
as the absolute right
answer, but L4 is clearly

00:35:18.658 --> 00:35:19.450
where the two meet.

00:35:22.450 --> 00:35:26.930
So if you apply that
basic mapping of the world

00:35:26.930 --> 00:35:31.720
back on to where we are
with the service mesh,

00:35:31.720 --> 00:35:36.130
you find an obvious parallel
between ONOs and Istio.

00:35:36.130 --> 00:35:39.190
And Onvoy is a
way of programming

00:35:39.190 --> 00:35:42.730
the data plane in the same
way that we can use P4

00:35:42.730 --> 00:35:45.707
runtime to program
the switching fabric,

00:35:45.707 --> 00:35:47.290
and, therefore,
affect the data plane.

00:35:51.318 --> 00:35:53.110
Now, that kind of leaves
us in a little bit

00:35:53.110 --> 00:35:55.027
of a struggle as to what
we call these things,

00:35:55.027 --> 00:35:57.370
because I've just added
an extra level here.

00:35:57.370 --> 00:35:58.810
So this is a control plane.

00:35:58.810 --> 00:36:00.850
Does that make this
the management plane?

00:36:00.850 --> 00:36:02.680
That is certainly
one interpretation.

00:36:02.680 --> 00:36:04.990
But the interpretation
that I prefer

00:36:04.990 --> 00:36:07.310
is to say that we have
a network control plane

00:36:07.310 --> 00:36:09.010
and we have a service
control plane.

00:36:09.010 --> 00:36:12.020
And the difference is, perhaps
subtle, but perhaps important,

00:36:12.020 --> 00:36:16.180
which is at the service level,
we do also keep track of state

00:36:16.180 --> 00:36:19.390
for the services that are
inside of the gray boxes

00:36:19.390 --> 00:36:20.470
at the bottom.

00:36:20.470 --> 00:36:22.990
And that's really critical
because we're not just

00:36:22.990 --> 00:36:27.640
managing a set of microservices,
or managing the functionality

00:36:27.640 --> 00:36:29.660
on behalf of subscribers.

00:36:29.660 --> 00:36:32.320
And this actually gets us to
one of the challenging problems

00:36:32.320 --> 00:36:35.380
in the telco case, which I
think has some applicability

00:36:35.380 --> 00:36:38.830
to service meshes in general.

00:36:38.830 --> 00:36:42.622
Which is that, if this
were a service mesh,

00:36:42.622 --> 00:36:44.830
and that's just an arbitrary
one, except, again, I've

00:36:44.830 --> 00:36:48.730
color coded it, so that there
are microservice based services

00:36:48.730 --> 00:36:51.010
and there are switch
based services.

00:36:51.010 --> 00:36:54.220
Then, I have, on behalf
of different subscribers,

00:36:54.220 --> 00:36:56.470
or classes of
subscribers, something

00:36:56.470 --> 00:36:59.710
called a service chain,
which is an instance

00:36:59.710 --> 00:37:01.038
of each one of those services.

00:37:01.038 --> 00:37:02.830
Let's assume they're
multi-tenant services,

00:37:02.830 --> 00:37:05.590
on behalf of a particular
subscriber, that gives me

00:37:05.590 --> 00:37:07.460
a path through the
service mesh on behalf

00:37:07.460 --> 00:37:08.710
of that particular subscriber.

00:37:08.710 --> 00:37:10.085
And I have another
service chain,

00:37:10.085 --> 00:37:12.430
perhaps it's different,
that runs on behalf

00:37:12.430 --> 00:37:14.540
of another subscriber.

00:37:14.540 --> 00:37:16.270
So in the networking world--

00:37:16.270 --> 00:37:18.640
in the access network
world, this is sometimes

00:37:18.640 --> 00:37:20.260
called network slicing.

00:37:20.260 --> 00:37:22.420
But I can give you some
guaranteed service,

00:37:22.420 --> 00:37:26.510
and I can give you customized
functionality on a subscriber

00:37:26.510 --> 00:37:29.110
by subscriber basis, or
perhaps on a subscriber class

00:37:29.110 --> 00:37:31.240
by subscriber class basis.

00:37:31.240 --> 00:37:35.260
This is really critical
for monetizing the edge.

00:37:35.260 --> 00:37:37.940
As you might imagine, it's
a pretty important thing.

00:37:37.940 --> 00:37:42.610
But as we roll out-- as
the telcos roll out 5G,

00:37:42.610 --> 00:37:44.860
the ability to control at
that kind of granularity,

00:37:44.860 --> 00:37:47.412
and not only provide
control at that granularity,

00:37:47.412 --> 00:37:49.120
but also diagnostics
at that granularity,

00:37:49.120 --> 00:37:51.070
and troubleshooting
at that granularity,

00:37:51.070 --> 00:37:55.190
is a critical requirement.

00:37:55.190 --> 00:37:57.920
So managing runtime
workflows, it's not just

00:37:57.920 --> 00:37:59.370
about configuration parameters.

00:37:59.370 --> 00:38:01.820
It's not just saying, here's
how I want you to behave,

00:38:01.820 --> 00:38:02.970
go for it.

00:38:02.970 --> 00:38:05.310
It's a runtime
operation as well.

00:38:08.360 --> 00:38:11.270
So the key takeaways.

00:38:11.270 --> 00:38:15.450
First of all, the access
network is being cloudified.

00:38:15.450 --> 00:38:19.500
It is being reduced to commodity
software, virtualization,

00:38:19.500 --> 00:38:21.930
disaggregation.

00:38:21.930 --> 00:38:24.390
The integration challenge
is the hard one,

00:38:24.390 --> 00:38:27.150
and that's the same
challenge that service meshes

00:38:27.150 --> 00:38:28.538
are targeted at.

00:38:28.538 --> 00:38:30.580
Some of the specifics are
a little bit different,

00:38:30.580 --> 00:38:33.038
but at the end of the day,
these service meshes, I believe,

00:38:33.038 --> 00:38:35.280
will span both access
networks on premises

00:38:35.280 --> 00:38:37.330
and back into the public cloud.

00:38:37.330 --> 00:38:39.510
Second big point is that
access networks will

00:38:39.510 --> 00:38:41.733
be part of the multi-cloud.

00:38:41.733 --> 00:38:43.650
There is a really, really
interesting question

00:38:43.650 --> 00:38:45.780
here as to who's
going to own the edge.

00:38:45.780 --> 00:38:51.540
And, in fact, if you look at
the two counter industries

00:38:51.540 --> 00:38:53.310
here in terms of
the cloud clearly

00:38:53.310 --> 00:38:58.050
moving towards the edge in
terms of the incumbent service

00:38:58.050 --> 00:38:59.490
providers are
already at the edge

00:38:59.490 --> 00:39:01.410
with the footprint in
the central offices,

00:39:01.410 --> 00:39:03.870
they're clearly trying to turn
that into a small edge cloud

00:39:03.870 --> 00:39:06.690
and move back upstream, so
there is a collision course

00:39:06.690 --> 00:39:07.770
happening here.

00:39:07.770 --> 00:39:09.810
I have no idea how the
dust is going to settle,

00:39:09.810 --> 00:39:11.630
but I do believe access
technology is going

00:39:11.630 --> 00:39:13.830
to be in a critical part
of that future multi-cloud

00:39:13.830 --> 00:39:16.038
because it's going to be a
critical part of the edge.

00:39:16.038 --> 00:39:18.000
And that's simply because
of the capabilities

00:39:18.000 --> 00:39:20.920
that 5G will
eventually bring us.

00:39:20.920 --> 00:39:23.762
There's a whole lot of
value that's potential there

00:39:23.762 --> 00:39:25.470
above and beyond just
thinking everything

00:39:25.470 --> 00:39:29.070
at the edge is
going to be Wi-Fi.

00:39:29.070 --> 00:39:30.870
The sub-points
given to that, this

00:39:30.870 --> 00:39:32.800
is certainly going to be part
of the telco infrastructure,

00:39:32.800 --> 00:39:34.633
but I think it's more
importantly also going

00:39:34.633 --> 00:39:37.590
to be democratized in part of
the on premises infrastructure

00:39:37.590 --> 00:39:39.060
at the same time.

00:39:39.060 --> 00:39:41.550
There is an
unlicensed band that's

00:39:41.550 --> 00:39:44.490
going to be available
for 5G in the US,

00:39:44.490 --> 00:39:47.430
and it's starting
to happen in Europe.

00:39:47.430 --> 00:39:50.190
Putting these capabilities
into manufacturing plants,

00:39:50.190 --> 00:39:51.540
parking lots.

00:39:51.540 --> 00:39:54.122
And anytime you try to
automate drones, or whatever,

00:39:54.122 --> 00:39:55.830
that's going to be a
critical part of it.

00:39:58.378 --> 00:40:01.820
And therefore, and it's because
of this intrinsic support

00:40:01.820 --> 00:40:05.210
for mobility, low latency and
scalable number of edge devices

00:40:05.210 --> 00:40:06.800
that this is going
to be critical.

00:40:06.800 --> 00:40:08.600
The final bulletin
is, I do believe

00:40:08.600 --> 00:40:11.912
that service meshes will be
the unifying abstraction.

00:40:11.912 --> 00:40:13.370
We already are
making good progress

00:40:13.370 --> 00:40:14.453
in two different settings.

00:40:14.453 --> 00:40:16.700
The telco one is a
slightly different one

00:40:16.700 --> 00:40:20.960
and a slightly newer one, but I
think these are going to merge.

00:40:20.960 --> 00:40:24.220
We clearly need to model
services and not just service--

00:40:24.220 --> 00:40:27.190
and be agnostic about the
surface implementation.

00:40:27.190 --> 00:40:30.500
When I say service in this
room, you immediately probably

00:40:30.500 --> 00:40:32.360
think Kubernetes
service, but we have

00:40:32.360 --> 00:40:34.190
to think more broadly than that.

00:40:34.190 --> 00:40:38.240
Second, support for fine
grain workflows at one time

00:40:38.240 --> 00:40:40.280
is going to be a
critical requirement.

00:40:40.280 --> 00:40:42.800
And there are clearly
parallels between what

00:40:42.800 --> 00:40:45.980
we're doing at L2, L3, and L7.

00:40:45.980 --> 00:40:47.300
We need to figure out--

00:40:47.300 --> 00:40:49.160
I think, in fact, it
probably comes back to,

00:40:49.160 --> 00:40:50.535
what is the language
with which--

00:40:50.535 --> 00:40:55.550
or the API with which I control
the data plane no matter

00:40:55.550 --> 00:40:56.510
what level I'm at.

00:40:56.510 --> 00:40:58.520
Because we're going to find that
functionally moving up and down

00:40:58.520 --> 00:41:01.100
the stack for performance
reasons and for generality

00:41:01.100 --> 00:41:02.555
reasons all the time.

00:41:02.555 --> 00:41:04.430
And that's where we need
to get ourselves to.

00:41:04.430 --> 00:41:05.330
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:05.330 --> 00:41:08.680
[MUSIC PLAYING]

